     ffsscckk__mmssddooss — DOS/Windows (FAT) file system consistency checker

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ffsscckk__mmssddooss [--ffnnppyy] _f_i_l_e_s_y_s_t_e_m

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffsscckk__mmssddooss utility verifies and repairs FAT file systems (more com‐
     monly known as DOS file systems).  It checks the specified filesystem and
     tries to repair all detected inconsistencies, requesting confirmation be‐
     fore making any changes.

     If the --pp flag is given, ffsscckk__mmssddooss preens the specified filesystem.  It
     is normally started this way by fsck(8) run from rc(8) during automatic
     reboot, when a FAT file system is detected.  When preening file systems,
     ffsscckk__mmssddooss will fix common inconsistencies non-interactively.  If more
     serious problems are found, ffsscckk__mmssddooss does not try to fix them, indi‐
     cates that it was not successful, and exits.

     The options are as follows:

     --ff      This option is ignored by ffsscckk__mmssddooss, and is present only for
             compatibility with programs that check other file system types
             for consistency, such as fsck_ffs(8).

     --nn      Assume a “no” response to all questions asked by ffsscckk__mmssddooss ex‐
             cept for “CONTINUE?”, which is assumed to be affirmative.  The
             filesystem will not be opened for writing.  This is the default
             for file systems to be checked that are concurrently mounted
             writable.

     --pp      Preen the specified filesystem.

     --yy      Causes ffsscckk__mmssddooss to assume “yes” as the answer to all operator
             questions.

     If neither of the --yy or --nn options are specified, the user may force
     ffsscckk__mmssddooss to assume an answer of “yes” to all the remaining questions by
     replying to a question with a value of “F”.

